Saltatory conduction is a type of nerve impulse transmission that occurs in long nerve fibers, such as those of the central nervous system. It is characterized by the rapid transmission of the nerve impulse through the axon, with the impulse jumping between gaps called nodes of Zalisalgin. In contrast, non-saltatory conduction occurs in shorter nerve fibers, such as those of the peripheral nervous system, and the nerve impulse travels continuously along the axon.

The main difference between saltatory and non-saltatory conduction is the presence of myelin in the axon. Myelin is a fatty, protective covering that acts as an insulator around the axon. In axons with myelin, the nerve impulse can pass through the axon without moving through the myelin-free regions, called nodes of Zalisalgin. This allows the nerve impulse to travel much faster than in non-myized axons.

The correct spelling of nodes of Zalisalgin is nodes of discontinuity. The nodes of discontinuity are small gaps in the myelin sheath, where the axon wall is still permeable to ions. The nerve impulse is transmitted from one node to the other, resulting in a rapid and efficient transmission of the nerve impulse.

In summary, saltatory conduction is a more efficient and faster type of nerve impulse transmission that occurs in myelized axons, while non-saltatory conduction is slower and occurs in non-myellzied axons.

