","The optimal ratio of titanium dioxide (TiO2) nanoparticles and graphene oxide (GO) in a composite material for use in high-performance dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs) can vary depending on the specific application and synthesis method. However, in general, a lower ratio of GO to TiO2 nanoparticles is more recommended. This is because GO can act as a charge transfer layer, facilitating the transfer of electrons between the TiO2 and the external circuit, which is crucial for the overall efficiency of the solar cell. However, if the ratio of GO to TiO2 is too high, it can lead to a decrease in the overall efficiency of the solar cell due to the increased recombination of electrons and holes.

To achieve the best performance, researchers often use a ratio of 1:10 to 1:5 (GO:TiO2) for the composite material. This ratio provides a balance between the electronic properties of GO and the light-harvesting properties of TiO2 nanoparticles. The optimal ratio can be further optimized by varying the size and shape of the nanoparticles, as well as the method of synthesis and post-treatment of the composite material.

The ratio of GO to TiO2 nanoparticles can also affect the stability of the solar cell. A higher ratio of GO can lead to a decrease in the stability of the solar cell due to the increased recombination of electrons and holes. This can result in a decrease in the overall efficiency of the solar cell over time. A lower ratio of GO to TiO2 nanoparticles can help to improve the stability of the solar cell by reducing the recombination of electrons and holes.

