","Isopopologues and isotopomers are two terms used to describe variations in molecules based on isotopic substitution. The key difference lies in whether the substitution affects the molecular structure or just the mass. 

1. **Isopologues**: Isopologues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \) are isotopologues because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the presence of deuterium (D) instead of hydrogen (H). Isopologues are chemically similar but can have different physical properties, such as chemical shift in NMR spectroscopy or infrared absorption frequencies.

2. **Isottomers**: Isotomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of their atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^{13}\text{C}^{13}\text{H} \) are isotomers because they have the same connectivity but differ in the presence of \( ^{13}\text{C} \) instead of \( ^1\text{C} \). Isotomers are chemically identical but can have different chemical shifts in NMR spectroscopy due to the difference in mass.

In summary, the key difference is that isotopologues can have different connectivity (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{D}_2 \)), while isotomers must have the same connectivity (e.g., \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}^{13}\text{C}^{13}\text{H} \)).
