","The recurrence relation for the number of comparisons in QuickSort in the best-case scenario is:
\[
C(n) = \begin{cases}
0 & \text{if } n = 1, \\
C\left\lfloor \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + C\left\lceil \frac{n}{2} \right\rfloor + n - 1 & \text{if } n > 1.
\end{cases}
\]
This recurrence is derived as follows:
1. **Base Case**: If the input list \( n \) has only one element, it is already sorted, so no comparisons are needed (\( C(1) = 0 \)).
2. **Recursive Case**: For \( n > 1 \), the algorithm splits the input list into two equal-sized sublists, each of size \( \lfloor n/2 \rfloor \) and \( \lceil n/2 \rceil \).
   - The number of comparisons for the first sub-piece is \( C(\lfloor n/2 \rfloor) \).
   - The number of comparisons for the second sub-piece is \( C(\lceil n/2 \rceil) \).
   - The number of comparisons for the comparison of the pivot with the elements of both sub-pieces is \( n - 1 \).
   - Adding these three terms gives the total number of comparisons for the current step.

The term \( n - 1 \) accounts for the \( n - 1 \) comparisons required to compare the pivot to all other elements in the input list. The other two terms represent the comparisons in the two sub-sets.
