","1. **Calculate the distance A travels before B starts:**  
   \[
   \text{Distance by A} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 10 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 30 \text{ km}
   \]

2. **Determine the relative speed of B with respect to A:**  
   \[
   \text{Relative speed} = \text{Speed of B} - \text{Speed of A} = 20 \text{ kmph} - 10 \text{ kmph} = 10 \text{ kmph}
   \]

3. **Calculate the time taken by B to meet A:**  
   \[
   \text{Time} = \frac{\text{Distance to be covered}}{\text{Relative speed}} = \frac{30 \text{ km}}{10 \text{ kmph}} = 3 \text{ h}
   \]

4. **Determine the distance covered by B when meeting A:**  
   \[
   \text{Distance by B} = \text{Speed} \times \text{Time} = 20 \text{ kmph} \times 3 \text{ h} = 60 \text{ km}
   \]

5. **However, this distance is from when B starts, so add the distance A has traveled to find the total distance from the start:**  
   \[
   \text{Total distance} = 60 \text{ km} + 30 \text{ km} = 90 \text{ km}
   \]

\[
\boxed{90 \text{ km}}
\]
