In summary, the addition of 4% copper and the formation of hard CuAl$_2$ particles during thermal processing will result in an aluminum alloy with a higher yield strength and a lower percent elongation compared to pure aluminum. This is due to the strengthening effect of the hard particles and the grain size effects associated with the thermal processing process.

