","The uncertainty principle states that the product of the uncertainty in the energy (\(\Delta E\)) and the uncertainty in time (\(\Delta t\)) is greater than or equal to \(h/4\pi\), where \(h\) is Planck's constant. Mathematically, this is expressed as:
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \geq \frac{h}{4\pi}
\]
For the given lifetime of the Σ⁺ particle (\(\Delta t = 2.0 \times 10^{-23}\) s), we can calculate the uncertainty in its rest energy:
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{h}{4\pi \Delta t} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{4\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}}
\]
Convert the uncertainty in energy to MeV (1 eV = \(1.602 \times 10^{-19}\) J):
\[
\Delta E \geq \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34}}{4\pi \times 2.0 \times 10^{-23}} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{1.602 \times 10^{-13} \text{ J}}
\]
\[
\Delta E \geq 1.7 \times 10^{-10} \text{ MeV}
\]
Thus, the typical range of outcomes for measurements of the Σ⁺ rest energy would be:
\[
1385 \text{ MeV} \pm 1.7 \times 10^{-10} \text{ MeV}
\]
The uncertainty is negligible compared to the rest energy, but the principle demonstrates that the rest energy cannot be measured exactly due to the uncertainty principle.
