The composition of the star also plays a role in determining its ultimate outcome, though to a lesser extent than the mass. A star with a higher iron content, for example, will have a lower binding energy and may collapse more quickly, leading to a more massive final remnant. However, the mass of the star remains the primary factor in determining the outcome of gravitational collapse.
