"An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).","<bos><start_of_turn>user
An amplifier has a voltage gain \( A_v = 100 \) without feedback. A fraction \( m_v = 0.1 \) of the output is fed back in opposition to the input (negative feedback). If the gain \( A_v \) falls by 6 dB due to ageing, calculate the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","1. **Initial Feedback Condition:**
   - The feedback gain \( m_v = 0.1 \) means 10% of the output is fed back into the input.
   - The closed-loop gain \( A_{vf} \) is given by:
     \[
     A_{vf} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v A_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1 \times 100} = \frac{100}{11} \approx 9.09
     \]

2. **After Attenuation:**
   - The gain drops by 6 dB, which means the new gain \( A_v' \) is:
     \[
     A_v' = A_v \times 10^{-6/2} = 100 \times 10^{-3} = 100 \times 0.3162 = 31.62
     \]
   - The new closed-loop gain \( A_{vf}' \) is:
     \[
     A_{vf}' = \frac{A_v'}{1 + m_v A_v'} = \frac{31.62}{1 + 0.1 \times 31.62} = \frac{31.62}{1 + 3.162} \approx 7.64
     \]

3. **Percentage Change:**
   - The percentage change in \( A_{vf} \) is:
     \[
     \text{Percentage Change} = \left( \frac{A_{vf}' - A_{vf}}{A_{vf}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{7.64 - 9.09}{9.09} \right) \times 100 \approx -13.2\%
     \]

   - The closed-loop gain decreases by \(\boxed{13.2\%}\).
