","The coefficient of induced magnetization \( \kappa_1 \) of a medium is affected by the presence of another medium with coefficient of induced magnetization \( \kappa' \) due to the interaction between the two media. The induced magnetization in the first medium is influenced by the magnetic field generated by the second medium, and vice versa. This interaction leads to a change in the effective permeability of the first medium, which in turn affects \( \kappa_1 \).

The exact change depends on the geometry of the system, the permeability of the two materials, and the strength of the applied magnetic field. In general, the presence of the second medium can either increase or decrease \( \kappa_1 \) depending on the specific conditions. 

For example, if the second medium has a higher permeability, it can enhance the magnetic field in the first medium, leading to a higher induced magnetization in the first medium and thus a higher \( \kappa_1 \). Conversely, if the second medium has a lower permeability, it can weaken the magnetic field in the first medium, resulting in a lower \( \kappa_1 \). 

In summary, the presence of another medium with a different coefficient of induced magnetization can significantly affect the induced magnetization of the first medium. The specific change depends on the specific conditions of the system. 

\boxed{\kappa_1 \text{ is influenced by } \kappa' \text{ due to mutual interaction of the magnetic fields, leading to a change in the effective permeability of the first medium.}}
